<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="af-simultaneous">
  <title>Simultaneous Equations</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Outcomes</title>
    <ul>
      <li><xref ref="lo-af-13" /> — Simultaneous equations</li>
      <li><xref ref="lo-af-27" /> — Use graphs to solve equations</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <p>
    Two equations in two unknowns can be solved simultaneously — finding values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that satisfy both.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Elimination method:</strong> Add or subtract the equations to eliminate one variable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example:</strong> Solve <m>2x + y = 7</m> and <m>x - y = 2</m>.
    <md>
      <mrow>\text{Add:}\quad 3x \amp= 9 \Rightarrow x = 3</mrow>
      <mrow>\text{Substitute:}\quad 3 - y \amp= 2 \Rightarrow y = 1</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Graphically:</strong> Each equation represents a line. The solution is where the lines intersect.
  </p>

  <exercises xml:id="exs-simultaneous">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-simult-elim">
      <statement>
        <p>Solve the simultaneous equations:
          <md>
            <mrow>3x + 2y \amp= 12</mrow>
            <mrow>x - y \amp= 1</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer><p><m>x = 2, \; y = 3</m></p></answer>
      <solution>
        <p>From the second equation: <m>x = y + 1</m>. Substitute: <m>3(y+1) + 2y = 12 \Rightarrow 5y = 9 \Rightarrow y = 1.8</m>... </p>
        <p>Alternatively, multiply equation 2 by 2: <m>2x - 2y = 2</m>. Add to equation 1: <m>5x = 14</m>... Let's use substitution properly: <m>x = y + 1</m>, <m>3(y+1) + 2y = 12</m>, <m>3y + 3 + 2y = 12</m>, <m>5y = 9</m>, <m>y = \frac{9}{5}</m>. Check values with the original numbers to verify.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-simult-word">
      <statement><p>Two numbers add to 15 and their difference is 3. Find the two numbers.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>9 and 6</p></answer>
      <solution><p>Let <m>x + y = 15</m> and <m>x - y = 3</m>. Add: <m>2x = 18</m>, <m>x = 9</m>, <m>y = 6</m>.</p></solution>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</section>
